{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_not_identically_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_not_identically_zero","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L216-L220","doc":"Negative control on the identity itself: the common spacing `−4b − 6c` is\n*not* identically zero, so `equal_spacing` is not the degenerate statement that\nall four masses coincide.  Witnessed at `b = 1, c = 0` over `ℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decuplet_hypercharges_distinct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decuplet_hypercharges_distinct","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L211-L214","doc":"Sanity: `equal_spacing` is a statement about four *different* states, not a\nrepetition of one — the decuplet hypercharges are pairwise distinct. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decuplet_casimir_is_affine_in_hypercharge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decuplet_casimir_is_affine_in_hypercharge","kind":"theorem","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L201-L209","doc":"**On the decuplet it *is* a function of the hypercharge**, and specifically\nthe affine one `casimir_collapses_of_isospin_affine` needs: every state\nsatisfies `4·I(I+1) = (Y + 2)(Y + 4)`.\n\nTogether with the previous theorem this is the contrast in full — the collapse\nholds on the decuplet and fails on the octet, both derived from the state\nlists rather than asserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.octet_casimir_not_function_of_hypercharge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.octet_casimir_not_function_of_hypercharge","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L189-L199","doc":"**On the baryon octet the Casimir is not a function of the hypercharge.**\nTwo distinct octet states share `Y = 0` while carrying different `4·I(I+1)` —\n`Σ` at `8` and `Λ` at `0`.  Hence no substitution `fourCasimir = g(Y)` exists\nfor the octet, and `casimir_collapses_of_isospin_affine` has nothing to act on:\nthe decuplet's collapse to an affine mass operator has **no octet counterpart**.\n\nThis is what stops the equal-spacing argument from being read as a generic fact\nabout `SU(3)` multiplets. The claim is derived from `octetStates`, not asserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decupletStates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decupletStates","kind":"def","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L186-L187","doc":"The decuplet as `(Y, 4·I(I+1))` pairs, same convention. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.octetStates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.octetStates","kind":"def","line":180,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L180-L184","doc":"The baryon octet as `(Y, 4·I(I+1))` pairs: `N(Y=1, I=1/2)`, `Σ(Y=0, I=1)`,\n`Λ(Y=0, I=0)`, `Ξ(Y=−1, I=1/2)`.  This list is *data* — the octet weight\ndiagram, imported, not derived here — and it is the only input to the theorem\nbelow. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.equal_spacing_cannot_discriminate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.equal_spacing_cannot_discriminate","kind":"theorem","line":159,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L159-L172","doc":"**The discriminating-power statement.**  Two arbitrarily different breaking\npatterns `(a,b,c)` and `(a',b',c')` — hence arbitrarily different physics —\ngive the *same* value of the equal-spacing combination, namely zero.  Equal\nspacing cannot tell them apart.\n\nThis is the decuplet analogue of `GellMannOkuboIdentity.gmo_cannot_discriminate`,\nand it carries the same consequence: reproducing the rule is not evidence for\nany mechanism. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:rem:decuplet-equal-spacing","label":"rem:decuplet-equal-spacing","md":"quantum-observable-universe/eightfold-way/decuplet-equal-spacing.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.second_difference_vanishes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.second_difference_vanishes","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L153-L157","doc":"The combination form: the second difference vanishes identically. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.omega_eq_two_xi_minus_sigma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.omega_eq_two_xi_minus_sigma","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L145-L151","doc":"**The `Ω` mass is not an independent prediction.**  It is determined by two\nof the lighter states alone: `m(Ω) = 2 m(Ξ*) − m(Σ*)`, identically in\n`a, b, c`.  So Gell-Mann's extrapolation extracts no dynamical information —\nit reads off a value already fixed by the ansatz's affine `Y`-dependence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.equal_spacing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.equal_spacing","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L131-L143","doc":"**The decuplet equal-spacing rule is an identity of the first-order octet\nansatz.**  All three consecutive spacings coincide for *every* `a, b, c`, over\nany commutative ring.\n\n`a` drops out (it is the common centroid) and the *pattern* of the breaking\nsurvives only as the single number `−4b − 6c`.  Two mechanisms with different\n`(b, c)` but the same `4b + 6c` are indistinguishable here, and *every*\nmechanism whatsoever satisfies the equality of the three spacings. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_omega_xi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_omega_xi","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L127-L129","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_xi_sigma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_xi_sigma","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L123-L125","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_sigma_delta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.spacing_sigma_delta","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L118-L121","doc":"The three consecutive decuplet spacings all equal `−4b − 6c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decuplet_isospin_affine","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.decuplet_isospin_affine","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L110-L114","doc":"The four decuplet states do satisfy that pattern. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.casimir_collapses_of_isospin_affine","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.casimir_collapses_of_isospin_affine","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L98-L108","doc":"**The decuplet's Casimir term is affine in `Y`.**  Whenever the isospin is\naffine in the hypercharge in the decuplet's pattern — encoded division-free as\n`fourCasimir = (Y + 2)(Y + 4)`, which is `4·I(I+1)` at `2I = Y + 2` — the\nquadratic combination `4I(I+1) − Y²` collapses to `6Y + 8`.\n\nThis is the whole mechanism: the `Y²` cancels, so the breaking operator becomes\naffine in `Y` and equal steps in `Y` give equal steps in mass. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.omegaMass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.omegaMass","kind":"def","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L93-L94","doc":"`Ω⁻(1672)`: `Y = −2`, `I = 0`, so `4·I(I+1) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.xiStarMass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.xiStarMass","kind":"def","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L90-L91","doc":"`Ξ*(1530)`: `Y = −1`, `I = 1/2`, so `4·I(I+1) = 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.sigmaStarMass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.sigmaStarMass","kind":"def","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L87-L88","doc":"`Σ*(1385)`: `Y = 0`, `I = 1`, so `4·I(I+1) = 8`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.deltaMass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.deltaMass","kind":"def","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L84-L85","doc":"`Δ(1232)`: `Y = +1`, `I = 3/2`, so `4·I(I+1) = 15`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.massQuadrupled","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DecupletEqualSpacing.massQuadrupled","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DecupletEqualSpacing.lean#L73-L82","doc":"The first-order `SU(3)`-breaking mass operator, quadrupled to stay\ndivision-free:\n\n    4 m(I, Y)  =  4a  +  4b·Y  +  c·( 4I(I+1) − Y² ).\n\nIdentical in form to `GellMannOkuboIdentity.massSqQuadrupled`; the decuplet is\nconventionally fitted with masses rather than masses-squared, but the identity\nbelow is insensitive to that choice — it uses only the shape of the operator. ","signatureChunk":0}]}